  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: CareDisc 3
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "In the waiting room, you\u2019re come across a flyer about a research
    team exploring new treatments for thyroid cancer. It mentions that scientists
    are reviewing caregiver reports to better understand what makes current treatments
    difficult for patients. Your friend, whose father is in treatment for thyroid
    cancer, turns to you and says, \u201CThis must be for a clinical trial \u2013
    researchers don\u2019t really seek out caregiver experiences until new treatments
    reach clinical trials.\u201D\n\nIs this true or false?"
  explanation: Caregiver input is valuable even in the earliest stages of research.
    Their real-world experiences help scientists understand which problems matter
    most, guiding early discovery work toward treatments that better support patients
    and families. This early insight can shape the direction of discovery, inspiring
    treatments that are not only effective but also kinder and more practical for
    those who need them most.
  correctAnswer: False
  incorrectAnswer1: True
  incorrectAnswer2: 
  incorrectAnswer3: 
  questionRole: Caregiver
  questionType: TrueFalse
  questionStage: Discovery
